            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"Offspring or descendants, specifically pointing typologically to Jesus Christ as the promised Messiah (the 'seed of the woman' and 'seed of Abraham'), and by extension, believers as spiritual descendants.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Genesis 3:15\",\"Genesis 22:18\",\"Galatians 3:16\",\"Romans 9:8\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"The Word of God or the Gospel message planted in human hearts to produce spiritual fruit.\",\"basis\":[\"Luke 8:11\",\"Mark 4:14\",\"1 Peter 1:23\"],\"prominence\":\"dominant\"},{\"meaning\":\"A small but genuine measure of faith that has immense potential for growth and power, akin to a mustard seed.\",\"basis\":[\"Matthew 17:20\",\"Luke 17:6\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"The physical human body that dies, is buried in the earth, and is subsequently resurrected in glory.\",\"basis\":[\"1 Corinthians 15:35-44\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"Financial giving, charity, or material offerings sown with the expectation of a spiritual or material harvest.\",\"basis\":[\"2 Corinthians 9:6-10\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"The biblical symbolism of a 'seed' is heavily context-dependent, primarily splitting into two dominant tracks: redemptive-historical (seed as the Messianic offspring) and parabolic (seed as the Word of God or faith). Both meanings enjoy universal consensus within their respective exegetical contexts. The financial interpretation is prevalent primarily within certain charismatic or prosperity traditions.\"}",
